{
  "reference_answer": "Honestly, not really — and the main reason is the pace. I've spent time in a couple of huge cities, and after about a week I start feeling like I'm constantly catching up. Everything's scheduled down to the minute: you queue for the train, you queue for coffee, you even book a slot to see a friend. What gets me is that you rarely get those unplanned moments that actually make a day memorable. In a smaller place, a quick errand turns into a twenty-minute chat with someone you bump into, and that's the stuff I remember. So it's not that I dislike cities — I just don't want to live at that speed. I'd rather visit for a few days and come home to somewhere I can breathe.",
  "reference_angle_summary": "独立主线：以“城市节奏太快、缺少计划外的偶遇”为唯一核心理由，用排队/预约与小镇偶遇闲聊作对比，最后落在“可以短期访问但不想长期居住”的条件性结论；示例不代表用户经历。",
  "takeaway_expressions": [
    {
      "expression": "I start feeling like I'm constantly catching up",
      "meaning_zh": "开始觉得自己一直在追赶、永远慢一步",
      "why_useful": "描述压力、节奏或工作负荷时非常自然，比 I'm stressed 更具体、更有画面感",
      "example": "By Friday I start feeling like I'm constantly catching up on emails.",
      "usage_note": "catch up 后接 on + 名词，表示追赶某类事务"
    },
    {
      "expression": "those unplanned moments that actually make a day memorable",
      "meaning_zh": "那些真正让一天变得难忘的、计划之外的时刻",
      "why_useful": "可用于谈论旅行、生活品质、城市与乡村对比，表达“意外之喜”的高频说法",
      "example": "What I love about travelling slowly is those unplanned moments that actually make a day memorable.",
